window.docContents[161].resolve({"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-copies-of-complete-graphs":{"contents":"copyCount is noncomputable, so we cannot simply evaluate it. But the unlabelled\ncopies of K[s] are easy to count: they are the s-cliques. Any two vertices in the\nimage of a copy of a complete graph are adjacent, so the copy is an embedding\n(Copy.topEmbedding). An embedding maps induced subgraphs to induced subgraphs\n(Subgraph.IsInduced.map), so the subgraph picked out by the copy is the subgraph\ninduced on its image (Subgraph.IsInduced.induce_top_verts).\n\nlemma Copy.toSubgraph_top (f : Copy (⊤ : SimpleGraph W) G) :\n    f.toSubgraph = (⊤ : G.Subgraph).induce (Set.range f) := by\n  -- the image of the induced subgraph ⊤ under f.topEmbedding is induced\n  have h := (Subgraph.IsInduced.top.map f.topEmbedding).induce_top_verts\n  exact h.symm.trans (by simp)\n\n\nSo a copy of the complete graph on W is determined by its vertex set, which is a\nclique with Fintype.card W vertices (isNClique_map_copy_top). Conversely,\nmatching the vertices of such a clique t with W (Fintype.equivOfCardEq) gives\na copy with vertex set t. To compare with copyCount, we use\ncopyCount_eq_card_image_copyToSubgraph, which counts the subgraphs f.toSubgraph\ninstead.\n\n#check copyCount_eq_card_image_copyToSubgraph\n\n\nIn a finite graph counting the number of copies of a finite complete graph on W is\nthe same as counting the cliques of G with Fintype.card W vertices.\n\ntheorem copyCount_top [Fintype W] [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V] [DecidableRel G.Adj] :\n    G.copyCount (⊤ : SimpleGraph W) = #(G.cliqueFinset (Fintype.card W)) := by\n  classical\n  rw [copyCount_eq_card_image_copyToSubgraph]\n  -- send a copy to its vertex set, and a clique to the subgraph it induces\n  refine card_nbij' (fun G' => G'.verts.toFinset)\n    (fun t => (⊤ : G.Subgraph).induce t) (fun G' hG' => ?_) (fun t ht => ?_)\n    (fun G' hG' => ?_) (fun t _ => by simp)\n  · -- the vertex set of a copy of ⊤ is a clique of the right size\n    obtain ⟨f, -, rfl⟩ := mem_image.1 hG'\n    simpa [Set.toFinset_range, map_eq_image, Copy.toEmbedding] using\n      isNClique_map_copy_top f\n  · -- the subgraph induced on a clique t is a copy of ⊤\n    replace ht := mem_cliqueFinset_iff.1 (mem_coe.1 ht)\n    let φ : W ≃ t := Fintype.equivOfCardEq (by rw [Fintype.card_coe, ht.2])\n    -- distinct vertices of the clique t are adjacent\n    have hadj : ∀ i j, i ≠ j → G.Adj (φ i) (φ j) := by\n      intro i j hij\n      exact ht.1 (φ i).2 (φ j).2 (by simpa using hij)\n    let f : Copy ⊤ G := ⟨⟨fun i => φ i, fun hij => hadj _ _ hij.ne⟩,\n      Subtype.val_injective.comp φ.injective⟩\n    -- the vertex set of f is t, since φ is a bijection onto t\n    have hf : Set.range f = t := by\n      ext x\n      simp [f, φ.exists_congr_left]\n    exact mem_coe.2 (mem_image.2 ⟨f, mem_univ _, by rw [Copy.toSubgraph_top, hf]⟩)\n  · -- a copy of ⊤ is the subgraph induced on its vertex set\n    obtain ⟨f, -, rfl⟩ := mem_image.1 hG'\n    rw [Copy.toSubgraph_top]\n    simp\n\n\nIn particular G.copyCount K[s] is the number of s-cliques of G. Since\ncliqueFinsets are computable, we can now compute copyCount for complete graphs.\nFor example, K[3][3] the 3-uniform blow-up of K[3] contains 27 triangles.\n\nexample : K[3][3].copyCount K[3] = 27 := by\n  rw [copyCount_top]\n  decide +kernel\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Algebraic Graph Theory\u0009Counting Copies*","header":"12.7.2. Copies of Complete Graphs","id":"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-copies-of-complete-graphs"},"/SimpleGraph/#section-the-simplifier":{"contents":"The simp tactic, Lean's simplifier, will be one of our most frequently used\ntactics, so it is worth knowing how it works. It rewrites the goal using the lemmas\nin the simp set, which consists of all the lemmas tagged @[simp] (including\nthose generated by @[simps]) in Mathlib and in the files we import. Each of these\nlemmas is an equation or an ↔, which simp uses to rewrite from left to right.\nIt simplifies the parts of a term before the term itself, and keeps going until no\nlemma applies. If the goal has become True (for example an equation whose two sides\nare the same) then simp closes it. The most common variants are:\n\n* simp at h simplifies the hypothesis h instead of the goal;* simp [foo, h] also rewrites with the lemma foo and the hypothesis h, while\nsimp [-foo] stops it from using the simp lemma foo;* simp_all simplifies the goal and all the hypotheses, using each hypothesis to\nsimplify the others and the goal, and is applied until nothing changes;* simp only [foo, h] rewrites with just the listed lemmas and hypotheses.\n\nsimp? works exactly like simp, but also suggests a simp only call listing the\nlemmas that were actually used (clicking on the suggestion in the InfoView replaces\nsimp? by it). Running it on the adjacency relations of our three path graphs gives\nthree different answers.\n\nexample (a b : ℕ) : pathNatFromRel.Adj a b ↔ a ≠ b ∧ (a + 1 = b ∨ b + 1 = a) := by\n  simp? -- Try this: simp only [pathNatFromRel_adj, ne_eq]\n\n\nexample (a b : ℕ) : pathNat.Adj a b ↔ a + 1 = b ∨ b + 1 = a := by\n  simp? -- Try this: simp only\n\n\nexample (a b : ℕ) : pathNatFromEdgeSet.Adj a b ↔\n    (∃ n, a = n ∧ b = n + 1 ∨ a = n + 1 ∧ b = n) ∧ a ≠ b := by\n  simp? -- Try this: simp only [fromEdgeSet_adj, Set.mem_ofPred_eq, Sym2.eq, ...]\n\n\nTwo things determine what simp does here: whether it can see inside the definition,\nand what it finds there. simp does not unfold a def (unless we ask it to, with\nsimp [pathNatFromRel]), so all it can do with pathNatFromRel.Adj a b is rewrite\nit using a lemma. It uses pathNatFromRel_adj, which @[simps!] generated for\nexactly this purpose. An abbrev, on the other hand, is unfolded by simp, and what\nhappens next depends on its body. The body of pathNat is a structure with its Adj\nfield written out, so simp reads pathNat.Adj a b straight off it, without using\nany lemma at all. The body of pathNatFromEdgeSet is fromEdgeSet s for a set of\nedges s, so simp sees (fromEdgeSet s).Adj a b and rewrites it with Mathlib's\nlemma fromEdgeSet_adj (and then tidies up the result).\n\nSo simp never uses pathNat_adj or pathNatFromEdgeSet_adj. They are still useful\nas named lemmas, for example with rw in the proofs above: pathNatFromEdgeSet_adj\nstates adjacency in a tidied-up form, whereas rewriting with fromEdgeSet_adj would\nleave a goal about Sym2.\n\nIn fact Mathlib would not accept pathNatFromEdgeSet_adj as a simp lemma. If\nanother simp lemma also applies to the left-hand side of a simp lemma, then\nsimp may use that one instead, and the lemma never gets used. So the left-hand side\nof a simp lemma should be in simp normal form: no other simp lemma should apply\nto it. The left-hand side pathNatFromEdgeSet.Adj a b fails this test, since the\nsimp lemma fromEdgeSet_adj applies to it. Mathlib checks every simp lemma for\nthis with its simpNF linter, which you can run on your own file with\n#lint only simpNF. We will meet simp normal forms again in Copy.\n\nsimp? has a second use. The simp set grows as Mathlib does, so a plain simp may\ndo something different in a future version of Mathlib. That's fine if simp closes\nthe goal, but if later steps depend on exactly what simp produced then the proof\nmay break. So the convention in Mathlib is that a non-terminal simp, one that\ndoes not close the goal, should be a simp only. Mathlib's flexible linter, which\nis enabled for this guide, warns about a non-terminal simp (its warning calls\nsimp a flexible tactic) and suggests using simp?. For example, if a + 1 and\nb + 1 are adjacent in pathNat then so are a and b, but the following proof of\nthis triggers the warning.\n\nexample (a b : ℕ) (h : pathNat.Adj (a + 1) (b + 1)) : pathNat.Adj a b := by\n  simp at h\n  exact h\n\n\nFollowing its advice and running simp? leads to a proof that does not depend on the\nrest of the simp set.\n\nexample (a b : ℕ) (h : pathNat.Adj (a + 1) (b + 1)) : pathNat.Adj a b := by\n  simp only [Nat.add_right_cancel_iff] at h\n  exact h\n\n\nOften a neater fix is to let simp finish the proof. simpa using e simplifies both\nthe goal and the type of e, then closes the goal with e (and\nsimpa [foo] using e also rewrites with the lemma foo). Without the simplification\nexact h would fail, since h and the goal only agree once both have been\nsimplified.\n\nexample (a b : ℕ) (h : pathNat.Adj (a + 1) (b + 1)) : pathNat.Adj a b := by\n  simpa using h\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009SimpleGraph\u0009Constructing a SimpleGraph","header":"2.7.2. The Simplifier","id":"/SimpleGraph/#section-the-simplifier"},"/Theorems/#section-tur-n-s-theorem":{"contents":"Mathlib calls a graph G Turán-maximal if it is (r + 1)-clique-free and has at\nleast as many edges as any other (r + 1)-clique-free graph on the same vertices.\nThis is a special case of IsExtremal, which says that a graph has the most edges\namong the graphs on its vertex type with a given property.\n\n#source IsExtremal\n#source IsTuranMaximal\n\n\nTurán's theorem P. Turán, Egy gráfelméleti szélsőértékfeladatról [On an extremal problem in graph theory], Mat. Fiz. Lapok 48 (1941), 436–452. says that for 0 < r the Turán-maximal graphs are\nexactly the graphs isomorphic to the Turán graph.\n\n#check isTuranMaximal_iff_nonempty_iso_turanGraph\n\n\nexample (n : ℕ) : T[n, 4].IsTuranMaximal 4 :=\n  isTuranMaximal_turanGraph (by decide)\n\n\nThe version of Turán's theorem that is usually quoted is the resulting upper bound on\nthe number of edges.\n\n#check CliqueFree.card_edgeFinset_le\n\n\nIn terms of the clique number ω (cliqueNum): a finite graph with ω G ≤ r has no\n(r + 1)-clique, so it has at most turanNumber n r edges.\n\nexample [Fintype V] [DecidableRel G.Adj] {r : ℕ} (h : ω G ≤ r) :\n    #G.edgeFinset ≤ turanNumber (Fintype.card V) r :=\n  CliqueFree.card_edgeFinset_le fun s hs => by\n    have := hs.le_cliqueNum\n    grind\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Theorems\u0009Turán's Theorem, Stability and Asymptotics","header":"13.2.2. Turán's Theorem","id":"/Theorems/#section-tur-n-s-theorem"}});